  "id": "Compound::DB01595 - Gene::2563",
  "verbalization": "Nitrazepam is likely to interact with Gabrd through a binding mechanism, as evidenced by its interaction with Gabra2, Gabra4, and Gabrq, all of which are subunits of the Gaba receptor. These receptors are involved in Gaba Receptor Activity, which is a key functional pathway in the nervous system. The presence of multiple binding sites for Nitrazepam on different Gabra subunits suggests a broad interaction profile, potentially modulating Gaba receptor function. The integration of these pathways implies that Nitrazepam may influence Gaba receptor activity through a common mechanism, possibly by altering the functional state of the receptor complex. This relationship is supported by the consistent evidence across different paths, highlighting a core interaction that links Nitrazepam to Gabrd via the Gaba receptor system.",
